/* v4 learner-facing rewrites: Verses (8/8 including v.what in base file) */
Object.assign(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY||(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY={}),{
'v.proph':{title:'Prophecy verses need history before they need prediction charts',dek:'A memorable prophetic line can sound timeless, but the first question is still: who said it, to whom, during what crisis?',body:['Prophetic verses are frequently quoted apart from the speeches and historical situations that give them force. A promise spoken to exiles, a warning to rulers, and a poem against injustice can all be turned into vague encouragement if the original audience disappears.','Start with the prophet’s setting: northern Israel or Judah? Assyrian pressure or Babylonian crisis? exile or post-exilic rebuilding? Then ask what the immediate passage is doing—warning, accusing, lamenting, promising, or using symbolic imagery.','Only after that should you trace later Christian interpretation or ask how the passage can speak into a new setting. Some prophetic texts receive explicit New Testament rereading; others are best applied through their moral/theological concern rather than a one-to-one prediction claim.'],plain:'A prophecy verse is first a message inside a historical crisis. Modern application comes after that context, not before it.',visual:{type:'verse-context',title:'Put a prophetic verse back in place',speaker:'Which prophet or prophetic voice?',recipient:'Israel, Judah, a ruler, exiles, a nation, a later community?',situation:'What historical pressure or covenant problem is active?',wording:'Warning, promise, lament, oracle, or symbolic vision?',application:'Trace later use or present application only after the first horizon is clear.'},challenge:{kind:'context-lens',title:'Restore prophetic context',prompt:'Build the strongest first-pass reading strategy.',fields:['First question','Historical clue','Literary task','Modern use'],options:[['Who first hears this?','Which headline today sounds similar?'],['Assyria/Babylon/exile/return','Only chapter number'],['Identify warning/promise/poetry/vision','Assume every sentence is a direct forecast of my life'],['Apply after establishing original meaning','Skip original meaning']],answer:[0,0,0,0],hints:['Original audience comes first.'],why:'Prophetic verses become more trustworthy when history, genre, and first audience remain attached.'}},
'v.love':{title:'Love verses become deeper when you ask what kind of love is being demanded',dek:'Biblical love language ranges from covenant loyalty and neighbor-love to friendship, family affection, erotic poetry, enemy-love, and community responsibility.',body:['A verse containing the English word “love” may translate different underlying words and may belong to very different contexts. Song of Songs celebrates desire. Leviticus commands love of neighbor. Jesus expands neighbor-love toward enemies. Paul describes communal love amid conflict. 1 John ties love to God’s character and treatment of other people.','Context keeps love from becoming a vague feeling. In 1 Corinthians 13, the famous language of patience and kindness sits inside a letter about a fractured church and a section about spiritual gifts. Paul is not merely writing wedding poetry; he is challenging competitive status and loveless use of gifts.','Responsible recall therefore attaches the verse to the kind of relationship and behavior it addresses. Love can include tenderness, but biblical love is repeatedly observable in patience, truthfulness, generosity, justice, forgiveness, and concrete care.'],plain:'“Love” is not one mood. Ask who is being loved, what behavior the passage requires, and what problem the command is answering.',visual:{type:'theme-thread',title:'Different settings for biblical love',stops:[{book:'Leviticus',title:'Neighbor-love',text:'Community ethics'},{book:'Song of Songs',title:'Desire',text:'Love poetry'},{book:'Gospels',title:'Enemy/neighbor love',text:'Love crosses expected boundaries'},{book:'1 Corinthians',title:'Community love',text:'Gifts without love become empty'},{book:'1 John',title:'Love and God',text:'Love becomes evidence of communal life with God'}]},challenge:{kind:'compare-board',title:'Keep love in context',prompt:'Sort the statements.',lanes:['Responsible','Needs more context','Misuse'],items:['1 Corinthians 13 addresses how people treat one another in community.','Every love verse describes romantic love.','The surrounding relationship helps define what love requires.','Quoting “love is patient” excuses avoiding truth or accountability.'],answer:[0,2,0,2],hints:['Biblical love is relational and behavioral, not only emotional.'],why:'Love verses gain meaning when their relationship, conflict, and demanded behavior remain visible.'}},
'v.strength':{title:'Strength verses are often about endurance, not unlimited achievement',dek:'Popular verses about strength can be misread as promises that faith unlocks any goal. Their surrounding paragraphs usually say something more grounded.',body:['Philippians 4:13 is the classic example. Paul says he can do all things through the one who strengthens him after describing need, abundance, hunger, plenty, and learned contentment. The “all things” is shaped by those changing circumstances.','Other strength language often works similarly. Biblical speakers ask for courage, endurance, refuge, and power to remain faithful. Strength can be received precisely because the situation is hard, not because the difficulty disappears.','That makes these verses useful in a different way. They can support resilient trust and community care without becoming a promise that every competition, business plan, illness, or desired outcome will end in victory.'],plain:'Biblical strength usually equips people to endure and act faithfully; it is not a blank check for guaranteed success.',visual:{type:'verse-context',title:'Philippians 4:13 in its paragraph',speaker:'Paul',recipient:'The church at Philippi',situation:'Support, need, abundance, hunger, plenty, contentment',wording:'Strength through Christ in “all things”',application:'Endurance and contentment across circumstances—not a guarantee of any chosen achievement.'},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'Strength or success slogan?',prompt:'Sort the claims.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['Paul discusses both scarcity and abundance around Philippians 4:13.','The passage connects strength with learned contentment.','The verse guarantees victory in any competition.','Hardship proves Christ has not strengthened someone.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,2]],hints:['Read verses 10–12 before verse 13.'],why:'The context frames strength as capacity for contentment and endurance across changing conditions.'}},
'v.fruit':{title:'The fruit of the Spirit is a character portrait, not nine unrelated achievements',dek:'Galatians contrasts destructive patterns with a Spirit-shaped way of life in community.',body:['The “fruit of the Spirit” in Galatians 5 appears after Paul contrasts works of the flesh with life by the Spirit. The list includes love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control in many English translations.','Paul uses singular “fruit,” which can help readers see the list as a portrait of Spirit-shaped character rather than nine independent badges. The surrounding chapter also emphasizes freedom expressed through serving one another in love rather than freedom used for self-indulgence.','Translation wording can vary slightly—especially terms such as faithfulness/faith or patience/longsuffering. Memorization should therefore stay tied to the active translation while interpretation stays tied to Galatians’ communal argument.'],plain:'The fruit list describes a Spirit-shaped way of relating, not a scorecard for proving spiritual superiority.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'From freedom to Spirit-shaped community',nodes:[{title:'Freedom',text:'Not slavery to self-indulgence'},{title:'Serve in love',text:'Freedom takes communal form'},{title:'Walk by the Spirit',text:'A different source of formation'},{title:'Fruit',text:'Character becomes visible'},{title:'Community',text:'The traits shape how people treat one another'}]},challenge:{kind:'match-board',title:'Recognize the fruit list',prompt:'Match the term with a plain-language expression.',items:['Patience','Kindness','Faithfulness','Self-control'],options:['Resisting impulsive domination of action','Durable reliability and loyalty','Active benevolence toward another','Enduring without quick retaliation'],answer:[3,2,1,0],hints:['Self-control governs impulse; kindness moves toward another’s good.'],why:'The list becomes meaningful when terms are understood as relational character, not decorative vocabulary.'}},
'v.speaker':{title:'Who is speaking can completely change how a verse should be used',dek:'The Bible includes narrators, God, prophets, poets, fools, accusers, friends, rulers, apostles, and even Satan. Quotation does not equal endorsement.',body:['A verse can be perfectly memorized and still be badly used if the speaker is ignored. Job’s friends say many confident things that the book ultimately critiques. Satan quotes Scripture during Jesus’ temptation. A Psalm may speak from despair. Ecclesiastes can voice a difficult observation inside a larger exploration.','Speaker identification is especially important in dialogue and narrative. Ask who is talking, whether the narrator supports or challenges the statement, and what response follows. Sometimes the book explicitly corrects a speaker later.','Recipient matters too. A command to a particular person in a particular role may reveal a broader principle without becoming a direct command to every reader. Speaker and recipient are the minimum context for responsible quotation.'],plain:'Before saying “the Bible says,” check who in the Bible is actually saying it—and whether the book agrees with them.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'Quotation is not automatically endorsement',nodes:[{title:'Words appear in Scripture',text:'Locate the quotation'},{title:'Identify speaker',text:'Narrator? God? friend? enemy? apostle?'},{title:'Identify recipient',text:'Who is addressed?'},{title:'Watch the response',text:'Is the statement affirmed, challenged, or left in tension?'},{title:'Then interpret',text:'State the meaning at the right level'}]},challenge:{kind:'scenario',title:'Check the speaker before quoting',prompt:'Choose the responsible move.',stages:[{prompt:'You find a striking line in Job. What first?',choices:['Identify whether Job, a friend, or God is speaking.','Quote it as God’s direct teaching immediately.'],correct:0,feedback:['Good: the dialogues contain disputed claims.','Reconsider: not every speaker in Job is endorsed.']},{prompt:'A speaker is corrected later. How should that affect use of the quote?',choices:['Include the correction in interpretation.','Ignore the correction because the first line is memorable.'],correct:0,feedback:['Good: later evaluation belongs to the context.','Reconsider: narrative response is evidence about the statement.']}],hints:['Dialogue requires speaker tracking.'],why:'Speaker and recipient prevent quotation from becoming decontextualized endorsement.'}},
'v.build':{title:'Rebuilding a verse from memory should rebuild meaning too',dek:'Word-order recall is most useful when chunks of language remain attached to the verse’s logic and context.',body:['A verse-rebuild exercise can become mindless if it only tests whether words were arranged in the exact sequence. A stronger exercise breaks the wording into meaningful chunks: claim, reason, contrast, promise, command, or consequence.','Reconstruction should follow understanding. If a learner knows that a verse has “do not be anxious” followed by prayer/thanksgiving and then a peace promise, the structure supports memory and interpretation at once.','Translation matters. Exact-word reconstruction belongs to a chosen translation; conceptual reconstruction can be translation-neutral. The app should tell the learner which kind of recall is being tested rather than treating one English wording as the inspired form.'],plain:'Memorize the logic as well as the words: what comes first, what supports it, and what follows.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'Rebuild meaning in chunks',nodes:[{title:'Opening claim',text:'What does the sentence start by asserting?'},{title:'Action / reason',text:'What response or explanation follows?'},{title:'Contrast / condition',text:'What changes the direction?'},{title:'Outcome',text:'What promise or conclusion finishes the thought?'}]},challenge:{kind:'verse-rebuild',title:'Rebuild the logic of Philippians 4:6–7',prompt:'Arrange the conceptual chunks in order.',items:['Do not be anxious about anything.','In everything, bring requests to God with prayer and thanksgiving.','God’s peace surpasses understanding.','That peace will guard hearts and minds in Christ.'],answer:[0,1,2,3],hints:['Instruction comes before the peace promise.'],why:'Reconstructing the conceptual structure strengthens both recall and interpretation.'}},
'v.all':{title:'Verse mastery means recall plus context plus restraint',dek:'The capstone skill is not memorizing the most lines. It is being able to recall, locate, contextualize, and use a passage responsibly.',body:['A mature verse profile includes reference, active translation, speaker or voice, recipient, literary setting, historical/community setting where relevant, immediate argument, major theme, and a responsible range of application.','That profile does not make application timid. It makes application trustworthy. A verse about anxiety can encourage prayer without blaming mental illness. A verse about strength can encourage endurance without promising victory. A judgment text can be taken seriously without pretending every disputed doctrine is settled by one English phrase.','The final discipline is restraint: know what the verse supports, what it may suggest, and what it does not establish by itself. Memorization becomes wisdom when confidence is proportional to context.'],plain:'Know the words. Know who said them and why. Know what they support—and stop where the evidence stops.',visual:{type:'verse-context',title:'The complete verse profile',speaker:'Voice/speaker',recipient:'First recipient/audience',situation:'Paragraph + book + historical situation',wording:'Active translation and conceptual structure',application:'Supported use with explicit limits'},challenge:{kind:'capstone',title:'Choose the mature verse explanation',prompt:'Which response shows whole-skill verse mastery?',clues:['The verse is memorized accurately.','Its speaker and recipient are known.','Its paragraph limits a popular overclaim.'],options:['Quote the line alone because exact words are enough.','Explain the line with speaker, situation, argument, and a proportionate application.','Avoid applying any verse because context makes meaning impossible.','Choose whichever interpretation is most encouraging.'],answer:1,hints:['Context does not cancel application; it disciplines it.'],why:'Verse mastery joins recall with contextual interpretation and evidential restraint.'}}
});
